<DocGuide chapter="library" title="Framework library guide" heading="Build your own client." accent="Keep the engine reusable." description="The library API, component composition and rendering lifecycle, independent of Lucent desktop preferences or Omarchy installation." sections={sections} audience="Library documentation">
  <RuntimeStatus />
  <section id="scope"><span class="eyebrow">01 / START AT THE BOUNDARY</span><h2>Four engine crates.<br />One optional design system.</h2>
    <p>The reusable framework consists of <code>lucent-api</code>, <code>lucent-ui</code>, <code>lucent-render</code> and <code>lucent-wayland</code>. Clients choose their own application state, components and service adapters. <code>lucent-design</code> is an optional set of tokens and recipes. None of the engine crates depends on the desktop client, its domain services or Omarchy.</p>
    <p>If you want to change launcher size, wallpaper, keybindings or startup, use <a href={withBase('/docs/desktop/')}>the desktop configuration guide</a>. If you want to implement a new client, start here and run the small example:</p>
    <CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked -p hello-layer" label="Inside a Wayland session with layer-shell and Vulkan" />
    <p>This is an in-workspace library. The repository does not promise a stable published crates.io API. The example is a real independent client, not an Omarchy service.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="masked-input"><span class="eyebrow">CONTROLLED SECRET INPUT</span><h2>Render a count.<br />Keep the value in the client.</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={'Element::masked_input(secret.len(), "Password", Message::Edit)\n    .id("password")\n    .autofocus()\n    .on_submit(Message::Submit)'} label="Generic framework control" />
    <p>The element stores a character count and placeholder. Layout paints bullets. Its callback receives InputEdit intents: Insert, Replace, Backspace or Clear. The framework never keeps the full secret in its input buffer or hit value; Debug output hides each edit's text.</p>
    <p>The client owns validation and actual storage. Lucent's network form uses a zeroizing domain Secret and moves it into an owned command. Cancellation, stale-form generations and retries belong to the client. The control knows nothing about Wi-Fi.</p>
    <p>on_submit works with ordinary and masked fields. Enter emits the supplied message, pointer input focuses, and Tab/Shift+Tab traverse controls and reveal scrolled fields. Transient event strings and external protocol copies are not guaranteed to be erased. Secure fields retain end editing; ordinary fields have the separate declarative text API below. Secure clients keep ordinary IME disabled. The merged text implementation adds explicit ordinary-client opt-in. Five owned native IME feature checks and five restoration checks pass; third-party IME acceptance remains pending, and platform accessibility is user-deferred.</p>
    <h3>Ordinary text has explicit selection state.</h3>
    <CodeBlock code={'Element::text_input(&self.value, self.field.selection(), "Search", Message::Edit)\n    .id("search")\n    .autofocus()\n\n// In update: use the current client value, not the painted snapshot.\nlet change = self.field.update(&mut self.value, edit, 8192,\n    ClipboardTarget::new("panel", "search"), effects, Message::Edit)?;\nif change.content { /* refilter or request a save */ }\neffects.redraw("panel");'} lang="rust" label="TextFieldState + relative TextEdit messages" />
    <p>The framework shares pointer, caret and selection geometry, supports grapheme/word navigation and uses relative messages for input bursts before the next frame. The component retains the string; cursor movement alone does not save preferences. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/text-editing.md')}>text editing guide</a> explains the complete state/input/rendering path and clipboard transfer and source-specific limits. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/text-shaping.md')}>international-text guide</a> describes the merged script/bidi/fallback path and ordinary IME adapter; Text <code>8e6d074b</code> retains hard-paragraph levels across soft wraps through exact row-bound <code>ResolvedBidiLine</code> in clonable <code>LayoutOptions</code>/<code>Paint::Text</code>. It uses line-local upstream L1/L2, preserves tab separator semantics and discards stale context before cache lookup. All six CI checks pass at that source, including the full workspace, controlled service protocols and 832 Vulkan visual cases. The owned native IME suite also passes its five feature and five restoration checks; third-party, physical and cross-output text acceptance remains pending. <a href={sourceUrl('lucent/examples/text-input/src/main.rs')}>The independent native example</a> uses the same API.</p>
    <p>Clipboard is opt-in through <code>Application::clipboard_enabled</code>. Ctrl+C/X/V queues an effect addressed to the live ordinary field; the native adapter negotiates UTF-8 and transfers through bounded nonblocking pipes on calloop. Completion returns to the owning child. Its ticket checks the current value and selection; native focus and surface lifetime are checked separately. <code>WritePosted</code> describes a queued selection request, not confirmed ownership. Wallpaper search and hidden-network names also use this control; wallpaper submission/provider changes retire old tickets, and network forms validate their generation and 32-byte name limit. Passwords remain separate count-only controls. Secure clients remain disabled. Read the guide for byte/deadline limits. <a href={sourceUrl('docs/multiline-text.md')}>Notes acceptance</a> includes single-output compositor copy/cut/paste with wl-copy/wl-paste and restart persistence; <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-gui-clipboard-2044.json')}>The later real Chromium/Notes run</a> passes nine keyboard-transfer checks in both directions and twelve restoration checks on unchanged <code>8e</code>, one virtual output. Rich content, other apps, delayed transfers and focus/seat retirement remain separate checks.</p>
  </section>
  <p>In the merged library, <code>Application::input_method_enabled()</code> and <code>Application::activation_enabled()</code> are independent opt-ins, each defaulting to false. Text fields keep the canonical String in the component; transient preedit stays in the runtime presentation. Fresh activation uses <code>Effects::activation_token(app_id, callback)</code> only during a physical input dispatch. Its app ID is an <code>Option&lt;String&gt;</code>: use <code>Some(app_id)</code> for a known application or <code>None</code> when an injected <code>UriPort</code> resolves the default handler. The client maps completion to the domain value <code>ApplicationLaunchContext</code> before calling <code>ApplicationPort</code> or <code>UriPort</code>. Concrete implementations such as <code>XdgApplications</code> and <code>XdgUri</code> are adapters; the framework never imports XDG launch policy. A token or successful command start is not proof of browser focus. Login, lock and authorization keep both capabilities disabled. These library contracts are separate from desktop settings.</p>
  <section><span class="eyebrow">A REAL PORT-DRIVEN CLIENT</span><h2>Notifications use the same component path.</h2><p>The desktop's notification child declares cards and grouped history with <code>Element</code>, controlled scrolling and <code>Motion</code>. Its state owns grouping, paging and reveal timing. The injected notification adapter owns native delivery and takes a separate history adapter. One bounded storage worker performs file I/O while snapshots, signals and expiry continue; completion versions prevent stale loads/saves from changing newer state. Renderer and Wayland crates know no notification IDs. Closing retires the surface after a settled submission, and interrupted transitions preserve their current value. Read the <a href={sourceUrl('docs/native-notifications.md')}>implementation guide</a> for action versions, archive recovery and the exact registration/update/render path.</p><p>The later <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-default-uri-native-26d.json')}>R9 default-route acceptance</a>, harness <code>563781a2</code> on runtime <code>26d03adf</code>, verifies native pointer and Tab/Enter notification input through <code>UriPort</code> and its <code>XdgUri</code> adapter: fresh context reaches the controlled handler and each route opens exactly one selected document in an already warm real browser. Five scoped checks, nine cleanup checks and independent later normal-OFF verification pass. The composition root supplies adapters; the framework supplies input-bound activation and generic effects. Active-window observations are separate from browser keyboard-focus proof. Automatic history release, a real browser receiving a key, fresh-browser initial focus and direct protocol-token consumption remain unverified in that runtime. Separate client fix <code>194c0c57</code> adds current-session-only history release and passes 372 desktop tests (six ignored) plus strict release Clippy; deployment and native browser key delivery remain pending. No performance or hardware result follows.</p></section>
  <section id="contract"><span class="eyebrow">02 / WHAT YOU IMPLEMENT</span><h2>State, messages and a view.</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(api, 'pub trait Component:', 'pub type Task<M>')} lang="rust" label="Actual Component and Application contracts" />
    <p>A Rust struct holds state. A message enum names possible changes. <code>view(&amp;self)</code> constructs an <code>Element</code> tree; <code>update(&amp;mut self)</code> changes state and records effects. An application also declares native surfaces, startup work and subscriptions. These are ordinary Rust method calls, not automatically discovered classes or registered DOM nodes.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>API value</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Element&lt;Message&gt;</td><td>Rows, columns, stacks, grids, text, images, inputs, sliders, bounded vertical scroll views, styles and interaction messages.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ViewContext</td><td>Current logical role and optional concrete instance, logical width/height, exact rendering density and monotonic time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SurfaceSpec</td><td>Role, optional instance and output target, layer, anchor, logical edge margins, size, reserved space, keyboard mode, visibility, pointer policy and optional redraw group.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Effects</td><td>Redraw requests, queued background tasks and exit requests. A redraw records intent; it does not submit GPU work.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Subscription</td><td>A stream or interval producer, keyed by an application-chosen ID, with cooperative cancellation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Motion</td><td>CPU interpolation between values at a supplied time. Duration and easing are selected by the client.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </section>
  <section id="compose"><span class="eyebrow">03 / A COMPLETE SMALL CLIENT</span><h2>Compose views.<br />Delegate messages explicitly.</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(example, 'impl Component for Example', 'fn main()')} lang="rust" label="Live hello-layer parent component and surface declaration" />
    <p><code>Element::map</code> wraps a child's messages in the parent's message type. <code>Effects::delegate</code> invokes the child's update and merges its effects, wrapping background results too. The runtime calls the application's update; it does not automatically locate and call an update method on every node.</p>
    <p>Use stable element IDs where focus, hover or input state must survive rebuilding a view. Each visible native surface gets its own view. Child coordinates are resolved relative to their parent's content box. A surface anchor is a compositor request; a row's padding is a layout instruction inside that surface.</p>
    <p>For a bounded card, <code>Anchor::TopLeft</code> requires explicit width and height; <code>SurfaceMargins</code> requests its position from the output edges. The runtime validates anchor/dimension combinations and unique IDs before native changes. The compositor decides actual placement. Output selection uses a separate optional SurfaceSpec.output with a validated OutputName. Missing named targets wait for registry availability; margins do not select a monitor. Fractional density has a separate optional protocol path. One-output live-video resize and integer/fractional-density retargeting pass in the <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-live-display-fit.json')}>native display-fit report</a>; simultaneous outputs, secure clients and hotplug remain separate acceptance.</p>
    <p><code>Event::OutputsChanged</code> publishes typed catalog observations through the ordinary application event/message/update path. The <code>hello-layer --output DP-1</code> example declares a target and consumes that catalog. The native backend resolves a current output object before creating its layer role and retires/recreates the role when its target changes. Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/output-targets.md')}>the output guide</a> for registry registration, waiting, closure, input lifetime, secure coverage and deferred native placement/hotplug acceptance.</p>
    <h3>One role can have multiple concrete surfaces.</h3>
    <CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked -p multi-output" label="Independent bars, counters and clipboard-enabled text per named output" />
    <p><code>SurfaceInstance</code> distinguishes copies of one logical role. The runtime keys native state by typed <code>SurfaceAddress</code>, while <code>ViewContext.instance</code> selects client-owned state. Capture that value in messages or use <code>Element::map</code>; two bars can reuse the same element IDs with separate focus and pointer capture. <code>Event::Scoped</code> preserves the concrete instance around native input/lifecycle events. Global output catalogs remain global.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(multiOutputExample,'fn surfaces(&self)','fn event(&self')} lang="rust" label="Actual independent client's repeated bar declaration" />
    <p><code>Effects::redraw_surface(address)</code> targets one instance; role/group redraws still reach every matching instance. <code>Application::animating_instance</code> reports per-instance frame demand, and <code>ClipboardTarget::in_instance</code> scopes field transfers to that exact native address. Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/surface-instances.md')}>the instance guide</a> for registration, routing, retirement and the native checks still deferred. The sample owns connected transient state. The separate <a href={sourceUrl('docs/monitor-layouts.md')}>desktop monitor implementation</a> composes this API over persisted domain profiles. Fractional density is implemented, and one-output live-video density migration has native evidence. The later desktop-based <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-multi-output.json')}>16-check native run</a> passes two simultaneous outputs, one owned headless, with real panel input at 1×/2×/1.25× and lifetime retirement/recreation. It does not repeat this independent example’s clipboard workflow or establish physical/cable/secure-output, suspend or presentation acceptance.</p>
      <p>A later desktop-client <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-two-output-displays-26d-nine.json')}>nine-flow Display run</a>, harness <code>1774fa87</code> on runtime <code>26d03adf</code>, covers draft/Apply/Revert/timeout/Keep, rotation, disable/re-enable and native pointer/keyboard input on the fractional peer panel. The panel uses 1024×768 logical units, density 1.25 and a 1280×960 buffer. Five positive preservation checks, one ctime/link-count scope marker, three cursor checks and independent later normal-OFF verification pass, including the same desktop lifetime and unchanged 14 source/43 security entries. Display transactions remain desktop domain/use-case/adapter policy; the library supplies scoped surfaces, density, buffers and input routing. This does not repeat the independent example’s clipboard test or establish physical monitors/cables, suspend/resume, compositor restart, secure-output changes, resource targets or presentation FPS.</p>
      <p>The later <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-4k-resource-preflight-followup-26d.json')}>4K A/B output preflights</a> verify a 2560×1440 logical background at density 1.5 with a 3840×2160 buffer and producer frame, then restore the original output. Both resource samplers refuse admission, so no resource interval was collected. A separate one-off diagnostic after restoration at FHD records 604.594 MiB process RSS, 539.586 MiB anonymous RSS, 16.242 MiB process swap and 680.293 MiB process-lifetime high-water RSS. These observations motivate memory investigation; they neither identify an allocator/decoder/renderer or GPU cause nor establish a leak, eligible budget, 4K causal effect or presentation rate. Framework density and geometry checks remain separate from desktop resource acceptance.</p>
    <p><code>Effects::recreate_surface(address)</code> renews an ordinary non-reserving layer-shell surface's native lifetime without changing its logical address or monitor routing. The runtime disables the old surface's pointer/keyboard input and retains one last-good presentation until its replacement successfully submits a frame. Repeated requests keep that successful presentation rather than an unpainted pending replacement. Native input, retry and frame events from retired objects cannot target the new lifetime. Logical frame counts and bounded timing/profile history continue; <code>native_frames</code> counts the current renderer separately. Hiding/removing the address, failure or shutdown releases both lifetimes. Positive committed, pending or replacement exclusive zones are rejected before native mutation; reservation transfer between surfaces has no atomic protocol guarantee. Secure session-lock clients reject recreation before executing the effect batch. This handoff adds no worker or polling; native compositor acceptance remains separate from the policy tests.</p>
    <p><code>Density</code> is a validated positive ratio, carried by ViewContext and Resize separately from integer output metadata. The native adapter uses fractional-scale plus viewporter when both are available. Layout and input remain logical; the GPU receives checked physical extents and actual per-axis clip sampling ratios. Text rasterization uses that density while preserving logical advances. Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/fractional-scaling.md')}>the density guide</a> for registration, fallback, ownership, frame submission and its original acceptance scope. Later <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-live-display-fit.json')}>single-output live-video checks</a> cover integer/fractional retargeting, without establishing simultaneous-output, secure-client or hardware behavior.</p>
    <p><code>PointerInput::Content</code> uses the tree's hit coverage; <code>All</code> covers the whole native extent; <code>None</code> lets pointer events pass through even when a decorative preview contains interactive elements. Keyboard policy is separate. An optional <code>redraw_group</code> lets one <code>Effects::redraw</code> invalidate related buffers; exact IDs still work. No extra redraw is scheduled while idle.</p>
    <p><code>Element::input_only()</code> retains a subtree's layout, focus and pointer capture while suppressing all its paint. Lucent uses this for stationary drag sources beneath a full-display preview. This differs from zero opacity, which removes the subtree from interaction. Widget grouping, spacing and drag policy stay in the desktop client; the framework knows only surfaces and trees.</p>
    <p>The example's slider is controlled by the client: <code>SliderSpec::new(value, step)</code> validates normalized values, and each <code>SliderEvent</code> returns the new value and whether the gesture finished. Its update stores that value and requests a redraw. Pointer capture, arrow keys, Home/End and focus belong to the framework; units and OS effects belong to the client. <code>Theme::slider</code> supplies semantic colors and spacing tokens. This demo does not control system audio. The desktop audio panel composes the same slider and scroll APIs over its injected AudioPort adapter; its native validation is tracked separately.</p>
    <h3>A scrolling list uses the same path.</h3>
    <CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked -p scroll-list" label="Independent wheel, scrollbar and keyboard example" />
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(scrollExample, 'impl Component for Example', 'impl Application for Example')} lang="rust" label="Live controlled scrolling client" />
    <p>The client stores an offset and passes <code>ScrollSpec::new(offset, line_step)</code> with one content tree to <code>Element::scroll</code>. Both values use logical pixels. Give the viewport a fixed height or the remaining height of a bounded column; give the content intrinsic or fixed height. Layout measures the content, reserves the scrollbar gutter, clamps the presented offset and places content at <code>viewport.y − offset</code>. The renderer receives ordinary clipped shapes and text; it has no scrolling policy.</p>
    <p>Wheel input starts at the topmost interactive hit and follows its actual scroll ancestors. It can reach an outer viewport at an inner limit, but it cannot scroll an unrelated list behind an overlay. A thumb drag captures the pointer until release, including outside the viewport. Removing the control or changing its measured range, track or clip cancels capture. Rebuilding with the same stable ID and geometry uses the latest callback.</p>
    <p>Tab and Shift+Tab move through reachable controls and reveal off-screen descendants. Nested reveal can return multiple messages, applied before one redraw pass. The focused viewport handles arrows and Home/End; Page Up/Down also work from its descendants. Ordinary clipped content and rounded corners with no native input coverage are skipped. Application-level key handling runs first; return no message for keys the framework should handle.</p>
    <p>Every scroll event returns a clamped offset. Store it and request a redraw; <code>Element::map</code> supports child message types. Layout does not mutate your saved offset when items disappear. This example resets its offset when replacing the data set; another client can preserve it and accept clamping. Scroll position has no persistence or OS effect unless the client supplies one.</p>
    <p><code>Theme::scroll</code> supplies semantic colors, padding, corner radius and generated <code>component.scroll</code> tokens. It remains optional. Views share immutable ancestor metadata, and keyboard traversal stops at the next reachable candidate. All list items are still measured and placed: this is bounded viewport scrolling, not a virtualized list. Touch, kinetic scrolling, horizontal scrolling and arbitrary programmatic focus/reveal remain separate work.</p>
  </section>
  <section><span class="eyebrow">INLINE CONTENT</span><h2>Formatted text stays reusable.</h2>
    <p><code>Element::rich_text</code> composes <code>TextSpan</code> values with optional registered font faces, color, underline and opaque link identities. Word wrapping continues across style boundaries; long words break at graphemes. Each line shares a baseline and becomes ordinary text/shape paint. Wrapped links share one keyboard focus target while their individual fragments define pointer and native input coverage. The engine never interprets a link as a URL.</p>
    <CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked -p rich-text" label="Independent formatted text and wrapped-link consumer" />
    <CodeBlock code={excerpt(richExample,'impl Component for Example','impl Application for Example')} lang="rust" label="A typed link action changes local example state" />
    <p>The notification client maps pure parsed content onto this control and uses its injected URI adapter for explicit link actions. The renderer preserves italic ink bearings while keeping layout advances stable. Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/rich-text.md')}>the rich-text guide</a> for layout, clips, focus, native input regions, fonts and current shaping/IME limits.</p>
  </section>
  <section><span class="eyebrow">MULTILINE INPUT</span><h2>The document belongs to the component.</h2><p><code>Element::text_area</code> takes a value, selection, placeholder, controlled <code>ScrollSpec</code> and edit/scroll callbacks. Use <code>TextFieldState::multiline()</code> to retain hard lines through the same bounded ordinary history and clipboard model. A shared source-indexed row map drives wrapping, pointer selection, vertical movement and visible-row paint; the engine retains only transient visual affinity and the horizontal navigation goal.</p><p>The native runtime refreshes CPU input geometry independently of GPU frame admission. Queued edits and row movement can therefore use current client state while a previous buffer awaits a compositor callback. Textarea painting emits visible rows individually, preserving raster density without creating one tall document texture. The current merged <code>8e6d074b</code> text path shares script/bidi/fallback shaping and source/caret geometry, with ordinary-client IME opt-in. Its owned native IME suite passes five feature and five restoration checks; third-party/physical/cross-output IME and broader pixel/scroll details remain separate acceptance.</p><CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked -p text-area" label="Independent controlled multiline consumer" /><p>Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/multiline-text.md')}>the multiline implementation guide</a> for model ownership, wrap affinity, capture, clipboard lifetime, runtime flow and 17 passing native Notes functional checks and nine restoration checks. That historical clipboard run used wl-copy/wl-paste. Later real Chromium textarea ↔ Notes keyboard transfers pass all nine interaction and twelve restoration checks on unchanged <code>8e</code> with harness <code>2044e54f</code>, one virtual output. Native pixel/scroll details, other apps, rich content and delayed-transfer/focus races remain separate acceptance.</p></section>
  <section><span class="eyebrow">ASSETS AND FRAME TIMING</span><h2>Images arrive through effects.</h2><p>The notification client requests current-version image content through its presentation AssetPort. Its adapter reads local files or converts validated raw pixels on the existing asset worker, prepares immutable ImageData with mip levels and returns typed results. One active batch and latest pending demand bound the queue. Views reference prepared data through Element::image; the framework knows no notification source path or hint policy.</p><p>A newly prepared picture receives a client Motion using design tokens. Its opacity is sampled at ViewContext::now, and active motion reaches the runtime through Application::animating. Compositor callbacks pace dirty frames until it settles; Vulkan blends the supplied value. Bounded scroll viewports keep image-card actions reachable. Read the <a href={sourceUrl('docs/notification-images.md')}>image implementation guide</a> for domain values, adapters, preparation, clips, input, retention and measurements.</p></section>
  <section id="overlays"><span class="eyebrow">04 / USE THE CURRENT GEOMETRY</span><h2>Anchor a label.<br />Own an explicit popup.</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={'theme.button("Choose", Message::Open)\n    .id("choose")\n    .tooltip(theme.tooltip("Choose a value", OverlaySide::Above))'} lang="rust" label="Optional Lucent design recipe over a generic Tooltip" />
    <p>The ordinary tree lays out first. A deferred overlay pass resolves the element ID against that surface’s actual bounds and clips. <code>AnchoredSpec</code> describes the preferred side, alignment, gap and viewport inset. Placement can flip and clamp; it cannot leave the native buffer or invent output-global coordinates. Missing, duplicate, clipped or invalid anchors cannot host the overlay.</p>
    <p>A tooltip adds paint alone. Its content cannot add focus, native pointer coverage or input handlers. Pointer dwell uses one cancellable calloop deadline across live surfaces; waiting requests no animation frames or worker. The short fade uses ordinary compositor-paced frame callbacks, then stops. Keyboard focus shows the label immediately. Escape, source retirement and pointer leave hide it. Client tokens supply width, spacing, dwell and transition policy.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={'root.popover(Popover {\n    id: "choices".into(),\n    anchor: "choose".into(),\n    placement: AnchoredSpec {\n        side: OverlaySide::Below, align: OverlayAlign::Start,\n        gap: component::popover::GAP, inset: component::popover::INSET,\n    },\n    content: Box::new(theme.popover(menu_content)),\n    dismiss: Message::Close,\n})'} lang="rust" label="The client owns presence, actions and dismissal" />
    <p>One interactive popover per surface owns a focus scope. The runtime refreshes its input geometry before native input even when a GPU frame is pending. Control keys reach that scope before application-global keys: Tab stays inside, Escape dismisses, and an outside press cannot release a control underneath. Closing restores the still-live opener; losing the anchor cancels capture and supplies a dismissal. Stable unique element IDs and client lifetime validation remain required. This initial API creates no native <code>xdg_popup</code>, secure-session role or nested menu system.</p>
    <CodeBlock code="cargo run --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked -p anchored-overlays" label="Independent tooltip, selection and focus-scope consumer" />
    <p>The <a href={sourceUrl('lucent/examples/anchored-overlays/src/main.rs')}>independent client</a> owns its opening <code>Motion</code> and selected value. Tray descriptions and dock names use the same tooltip API in the desktop. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/anchored-overlays.md')}>implementation guide</a> follows placement, lifecycle and the deliberate one-scope limit. Strict checks pass 717 regular Rust tests and 103 Python checks; all 712 software-Vulkan comparisons pass with 24 reviewed new references. Production bar/dock pointer checks and four static native captures also pass on one 1× output, with exact caller-state preservation. The <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-anchored-overlays.json')}>scoped acceptance report</a> retains those results and limits. The later <a href={withBase('/component-lab/')}>independent component lab</a> at <code>b9de7ba4</code> passes native popover opening focus, Escape dismissal and opener-focus restoration on one prepared output. Native outside-click dismissal, keyboard focus trapping, popover clipboard interoperability, bar click-through, precise fade timing, smoothness and current RSS still need their own evidence.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="execute"><span class="eyebrow">05 / WHEN THINGS HAPPEN</span><h2>One event loop owns state.</h2>
    <p>The executable selects the runtime entry point. <code>run</code> enables the local command and inspection server; <code>run_without_ipc</code> runs ordinary Wayland surfaces with that server disabled. Permission dialogs use this explicit mode, and <code>run_greeter</code> delegates to it for login. Secure lock clients use <code>run_locked</code>, which protects every output through the session-lock protocol and requires the client's authenticated result before unlocking.</p>
    <p>Returning from a runtime invocation also tears down its native surfaces. The runtime drops each GPU surface before its Wayland role, then flushes queued destruction requests while the connection is alive. This matters for a permission agent that remains running after an individual dialog closes. The same cleanup runs on error returns; destroying presentation resources never authorizes a session unlock.</p>
    <CodeBlock code={`run(application)
  connect to Wayland; create shared GPU and layout resources
  register event sources with calloop
  call init; reconcile surfaces and subscriptions
  wait for events; dispatch the available batch

inside dispatch: message → update → effects → invalidate surface
after dispatch, once per drawable dirty surface:
configured + dirty + no pending frame
  → view → layout → paint and hit regions
  → wgpu/Vulkan commands → present buffer
Wayland frame callback → allow the next dirty frame`} label="Simplified implementation; not copyable Rust" />
    <p>Pointer and keyboard events arrive through Wayland. An optional local command socket translates external strings into application messages. Effects run on named ordered queues; independent queues and subscription workers can run concurrently. All return messages to the event loop, which owns the application state.</p>
    <p>GPU painting happens once after the available event batch, using the resulting state. Input layout remains synchronous, so another click or key uses current controls while a compositor callback is pending. A successful submission can request one coalesced wake for newly drawable followup work; idle or callback-blocked surfaces do not poll. Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/event-batches.md')}>the event-batching guide</a> for input capture, submission handoffs and frame admission.</p>
    <p><code>Effects::task_on_with_failure(queue, run, on_failure)</code> attaches a small message constructor to the specific operation. Rejection or panic returns its own typed result even when several features share a queue; <code>Effects::delegate</code> lifts both success and failure. The mapper must not block because rejected work is handled directly on the UI thread. Cancellation skips it. Without a mapper, or if the mapper panics, <code>Application::runtime_failure</code> handles the safe failure. The desktop declares eight lanes in <code>work.rs</code>; pairing answers share the short controls lane while native methods use their own lane.</p>
    <p>Background delivery holds at most 64 waiting messages. A full queue pauses its producer; it never blocks the UI thread to enqueue. Subscription cancellation releases that wait and invalidates already queued events from the old lifetime. The runtime admits at most 64 live subscription bodies, including streams still stopping. Do not hold a lock needed by the UI while emitting. Payload sizes and service reconnection remain application/adapter responsibilities.</p>
    <p><code>Subscription::map(ParentMessage::Child)</code> lifts an injected child's stream into its parent without creating another worker. Its identity, cancellation and bounded emitter remain the same lifetime. For an operation that must cancel and report admission failure, use <code>Effects::task_cancellable_on_with_failure</code>. Lucent's media import uses this contract so closing retires pending delivery and queue rejection clears the specific import ticket.</p>
    <p>The renderer owns shaders, primitive buffers, image/text caches and GPU submission. Hyprland combines our buffers with other windows. The compositor does not receive the Element tree. Frame callbacks pace further work; an idle surface does not redraw continuously.</p>
    <p><code>ImageData::from_rgba</code> prepares premultiplied color pixels. For monochrome artwork, <code>ImageData::from_alpha</code> prepares one coverage byte per pixel; the element's tint supplies its color. Both constructors prepare the same mip dimensions and area-filter rounding on an asset worker. <code>format()</code> identifies the storage, and <code>ImageLevel.pixels</code> contains tightly packed bytes in that format. This field replaces the earlier RGBA-only <code>rgba</code> name in the pre-release API.</p>
    <p>Changing pixels use <code>ImageStream</code>. Construct one stream for a producer, then call <code>frame_rgba(width, height, bytes)</code> on that worker. Each returned <code>ImageData</code> owns immutable premultiplied bytes and one image level. Its allocation identity stays distinct from the stream's GPU resource identity and revision. The renderer uploads a changed revision into the existing texture when dimensions match; different dimensions replace it. A repeated revision needs no upload. Nothing in this API discovers files, decodes video, selects an output or schedules a wallpaper.</p>
    <p><code>ImageData::lifetime()</code> returns an <code>ImageLifetime</code>: a weak observation of the shared pixel allocation, without retaining pixels. Value clones and frozen snapshots keep it alive; a copied, smaller mip chain has its own lifetime. The renderer uses this observation to discard an unused image texture when its source has expired, even below the cache's reuse budget. Every canvas still pins its current draw resources, so another output cannot evict them. Stream updates replace the observation with the latest frame's lifetime; reusable text resources keep their normal cache policy. Cleanup happens during existing draws and canvas teardown, with no extra idle polling. Texture payload retirement does not guarantee an immediate decrease in process RSS or driver allocations.</p>
    <p>The desktop's <a href={sourceUrl('docs/live-wallpapers.md')}>native media guide</a> follows a real consumer from saved domain preferences through an injected playback adapter, a latest-frame mailbox, exact background invalidation, Vulkan upload and Wayland submission. This demonstrates why a producer event and a compositor frame callback are separate: the former changes content; the latter permits presentation of dirty work. A settled panel's submission event requests no further paint. Automatic battery/fullscreen pauses are pure domain/use-case decisions. The client joins port observations, retains a static image snapshot for a paused output, and keeps a shared producer only while another output needs it; the framework renderer has no wallpaper policy.</p>
    <p>The Vulkan adapter uploads coverage as <code>R8Unorm</code> and reconstructs premultiplied white before applying tint and opacity. Text also uses a single coverage channel, keeping its existing 2× supersampling and output density. This reduces each mask's CPU and texture payload by 75%; it does not reduce its resolution. Color icons and wallpapers remain RGBA. Payload savings do not establish a process RSS improvement or total GPU-memory budget; native measurements cover those separately.</p>
    <p>The shared GPU device requests <a href="https://docs.rs/wgpu/30.0.1/wgpu/enum.MemoryHints.html"><code>MemoryHints::MemoryUsage</code></a> to favor memory use over allocation throughput. This supported backend preference suits a UI with small scenes and long idle periods. It changes allocation strategy, not surface size or image density. A backend may ignore the hint; it does not cap active resource sizes, driver allocations or process RSS. Hardware frame pacing needs separate measurements.</p>
    <p>A successful submission emits <code>Event::FrameSubmitted</code> after the surface's scene and input bookkeeping. It carries that buffer's logical width/height and the monotonic time used by its view. The application can turn it into a message; returning no message requests no extra redraw. This event confirms our submission, not GPU completion or display scanout. A Wayland frame callback separately permits the next dirty frame.</p>
    <p>The desktop uses that event to hand the shared bar between native panel hosts. Client state tracks each output's previous and requested carrier, keeps outgoing controls inert and preserves the 48-pixel reservation. The framework still receives ordinary component trees and surface declarations. This <a href={sourceUrl('docs/panel-flow.md')}>client presentation policy</a> does not make separate Wayland commits atomic or acknowledge scanout.</p>
    <p>Keep display layout separate from native buffer size. <code>ViewContext</code> and input hits use surface-local coordinates. The desktop's settled dock requests a short bottom-anchored buffer, translates its existing display-grid position into that buffer, expands before opening and shrinks after a settled closing submission. Those choices belong to the client. <code>Shadow::blur_outset</code> exposes the renderer's finite shadow support so clients can retain all painted pixels when calculating a crop; it does not select a desktop's spacing tokens.</p>
    <p>Logical dimensions become physical texture dimensions through checked multiplication by the current integer scale. Native surfaces and <code>Canvas::snapshot</code> reject dimensions beyond the requested GPU device's texture limit; snapshots also check aligned readback size and reserve their packed result fallibly. Device limits describe valid requests, not available memory. Fractional scaling remains separate work.</p>
    <p><code>Event::Resize</code> carries logical width/height, integer fallback scale and the actual validated <code>Density</code>. It arrives after surface configuration and when a configured surface's density changes. Clients use the density's checked physical-size conversion for display assets; layout and input remain logical. <code>ImageData::downsample_for_cover</code> copies only the coarsest prepared mip chain whose two dimensions cover that physical target. It preserves the original aspect through <code>aspect_size()</code>, even when odd mip sizes round down. Run this preparation on an asset worker. The framework neither reads a wallpaper file nor decides when a desktop selection commits.</p>
    <p><code>Canvas::paint</code> returns a result. The renderer validates scene geometry, image dimensions, text requests and clip-buffer capacity, then prepares resources before writing geometry or submitting a frame. A rejected scene retains the canvas's previous resources and target pixels; temporary uploads become eligible for cache eviction. This library behavior does not add automatic native recovery: the runtime currently treats a rendering error as fatal and follows its existing supervision policy.</p>
    <p>Renderer admission limits are backend constraints, separate from design tokens: coordinates and numeric effects use a finite 2²⁴ envelope, text primitives accept at most 64 KiB of UTF-8, and supersampled glyph bitmaps are bounded before rasterization. Geometry buffers use the requested device's limits, including the storage-binding limit for clips. These checks neither cap total process memory nor validate every upstream layout operation or font parser.</p>
    <p>For CPU-stage evidence, start the runtime with <code>LUCENT_PROFILE_FRAMES=1</code>. Inspection keeps the latest 120 successful frame samples per surface: view, layout, native input, buffer acquisition, preparation, encoding, submission and presentation calls. Text rasterization and uploads are nested within preparation. These are CPU wall times including driver waits; they are not GPU timestamps or display latency. Profiling is off by default and requests no extra frames.</p>
    <p>The same opt-in also exposes a separate, bounded <code>frame_pacing</code> trace: existing callback requests and handler entry, raw callback timestamps, and every admitted draw attempt with a fixed outcome. Native retirement discards pending timing authority; overlapping requests make correlation ambiguous rather than joining a callback to the newest request. The <a href={sourceUrl('docs/frame-callback-timing.md')}>timing guide</a> covers the 512-event limit, missing/retired counters and unchanged successful-frame schema. This is private backend instrumentation: domain ports, client state and rendering policy are unchanged, with no added redraw or timer.</p>
    <p>A separate <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-native-callback-timing.json')}>native diagnostic at <code>91b964ae</code></a> passes four execution and five restoration checks. Its mean request-to-handler interval is 44.175852 ms, handler-to-next-attempt 0.006112 ms and attempt duration 1.603372 ms; compositor feedback separately records 21.972535 intervals/s. Request-to-handler includes transport, scheduling and dispatch, so it is not compositor-only time. Process-relative nanoseconds, raw core callback milliseconds and presentation clock IDs remain separate. This main-based integration copies the diagnostic and timing code without the unaccepted renderer/video ancestors; its source and CI/delivery identities are separate from the <code>91b</code> native run, and those checks do not extend that native scope. No causal or performance-target acceptance follows.</p>
    <p>Read <a href={withBase('/docs/framework/')}>the detailed library implementation</a> for layout, clipping, input, textures and scheduling. <a href={withBase('/docs/execution/')}>Follow the wiring</a> traces this same engine through the actual desktop client.</p>
  </section>
  <p>Shared input recipes in <code>lucent-design</code> give ordinary fields a 48-pixel height, 16-pixel horizontal inset and 8-pixel vertical inset. Use <code>Theme::text_input</code> for a complete field, or <code>input_group</code> with <code>text_input_content</code> for leading icons and trailing buttons. The layout engine calculates caret positions from the same font metrics as the text. Placeholder dimming comes from the design token <code>component.input.placeholder_opacity</code>; the reusable SDK’s explicit style defaults to full opacity. Authentication uses count-only masked inputs for secrets; the client retains zeroizing storage.</p>
  <p>Click interception and hover feedback are separate. A panel can consume outside-click routing while setting <code>hover_feedback(false)</code>; its child buttons still highlight. Wallpaper crossfades belong to the desktop presentation component. It starts SDK Motion after the first frame for a new selection submits, so that frame's wait and rendering time do not consume the fade. Image opacity follows the timeline; at most one outgoing frame stays retained per output until a fully revealed view submits. Submission does not acknowledge compositor scanout. <code>ImageData::snapshot</code> freezes a streamed frame by sharing immutable pixels under a separate GPU identity. Vulkan draws each supplied opacity; the client timeline interpolates it.</p>
  <p>Pointer actions use <code>on_click</code>, <code>on_secondary_click</code>, <code>on_middle_click</code> and <code>on_wheel</code>. The SDK owns topmost clipped hit testing and matching-button capture. Moving away, leaving the surface, removing the handler or changing its geometry retires alternate-button capture. <code>WheelEvent</code> carries horizontal and vertical logical lines, including fractions. A leaf handler consumes the event before ancestor scrolling. <code>Element::map</code> lifts these callbacks like any other child message; <code>Effects::delegate</code> keeps asynchronous work in the child. For the tray, the path is Wayland input → SDK interaction → Tray child message → injected <code>TrayPort</code> → StatusNotifier adapter → application D-Bus method. The engine knows nothing about tray applications or menus.</p>
  <section id="extend"><span class="eyebrow">06 / CHANGE THE RIGHT LAYER</span><h2>Keep product policy in clients.</h2>
    <p>Launcher sizing, widget snapping and Omarchy integration belong to the desktop. Generic layout constraints belong to <code>lucent-ui</code>; new surface protocol support belongs to <code>lucent-wayland</code>; texture sampling belongs to <code>lucent-render</code>. A service contract is a port. Its implementation is an adapter. Presentation data such as decoded image pixels belongs outside the pure desktop domain.</p>
    <CodeBlock code="cargo fmt --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --all --check\ncargo clippy --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --manifest-path lucent/Cargo.toml --locked --workspace\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v" label="Boundary and behavior checks" />
    <p>Use <a href={sourceUrl('.agents/skills/lucent-framework/SKILL.md')}>the repository's lucent-framework skill</a> for library or architecture work. Scoped owned native tests pass IME composition/navigation and two-output density/input/lifetime handling. Real Chromium/Notes keyboard clipboard transfer now passes in both directions on one virtual output, with nine interaction and twelve restoration checks. Current limits include third-party IMEs, other clipboard apps/rich content/transfer races, physical/secure output changes and eligible performance measurements. Accessibility integration is user-deferred. One-output live-video density retargeting and Notes editing/clipboard/persistence retain their own scoped native evidence. Read <a href={sourceUrl('docs/current-readiness.md')}>the readiness guide</a> before treating historical pending entries as the current summary. The <a href={withBase('/docs/review/')}>architecture review</a> separates checked boundaries from remaining design work.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="weather-client"><h2>A service child uses the existing SDK.</h2><p>Weather is desktop code built with the library. The executable constructs an <code>OpenMeteo</code> adapter, injects it through the domain <code>WeatherPort</code>, and creates the Weather child. The root explicitly wraps its subscriptions and messages; the engine does not discover or register service objects automatically.</p><p>The child composes ordinary <code>TextFieldState</code>/<code>TextEdit</code> inputs, controlled scrolling, generated design recipes and <code>Motion</code>. Editing changes its draft. Apply returns a preference intent to the root's persistence coordinator. Closing retires form and clipboard authority. Layout, input, Wayland and Vulkan contain no forecast policy.</p><p>A generation identifies the existing subscription slot. Its producer sends Started/Observed, calls the adapter away from the UI thread, then sleeps between refreshes. A stable ID retains that producer. Idle location changes or explicit refreshes start a new generation; while a forecast request is busy, they coalesce into one latest demand until the bounded request completes. Old-location presentation clears immediately, and replies for coordinates no longer desired are ignored. Local behavior, reviewed visuals and scoped native opening/focus checks pass. One configured production forecast and a current resource sample are recorded; form changes, failure/retry, resource targets and pacing remain open. Follow the <a href={sourceUrl('docs/weather.md')}>startup-to-frame guide</a> and <a href={sourceUrl('reports/measurements/lucent-weather.json')}>scoped evidence</a>.</p></section>
